# FACTORY (the author, 2026-10-05): `@: h f(a)` receives a factory's result; this row wrote `h: f a` until then.
# A method that returns a nested definition builds its node where its own
# body ends, not where a nested body of it ends. makeAdder counts in a
# loop, adding 1 to n three times, takes n back to 0 when it is above 100,
# and returns addN, which reads n. makeAdder 5 holds 8: 8 + 1. makeAdder
# 200 holds 0: 0 + 1. Built at the end of the loop's body, the node held 6
# and gave 7. Entry 7.
fn: makeAdder (int: n) fn: (int: x) int
    int: i 0
    while: i < 3
        n: n + 1
        i: i + 1
    ---
    if: n > 100
        n: 0
    fn: addN (int: x) int
        return: n + x
    return: addN

@: add5 makeAdder(5)
@: add0 makeAdder(200)
int: a (add5: 1)
int: b (add0: 1)
int: ok 7
if: a != 9 || b != 1
    ok: 81
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: ok; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
